"## Carbon monoxide poisoning theory for the Dyatlov Pass incident: proponents, supporting evidence, criticisms, and how Wikipedia and investigators treat it\n# Carbon Monoxide Theory — Dyatlov Pass Incident (Feb 1959)\n\n## The theory\nThat carbon monoxide — most commonly attributed to the group's portable tent stove (backdraft, faulty chimney draft, or fume accumulation) — caused headache, confusion, hallucination, and panic, driving the nine hikers to cut their way out of the tent and flee down the slope without proper clothing or boots [1][5][7].\n\n## Proponents\n- No single academic champion; unlike the avalanche theory (Gaume & Puzrin, 2021) or infrasound theory (Donnie Eichar's *Dead Mountain*, 2013) [2][6], the CO hypothesis circulates mainly among Russian amateur research circles (e.g., contributors to the Dyatlov Foundation's hypothesis catalogue) and is periodically revived in popular media around anniversaries and news cycles [5][7].\n- It appeals to \"natural causes\" advocates because it explains the panicked flight without invoking crime, military tests, or paranormal forces.\n\n## Supporting evidence cited by proponents\n- The tent was equipped with a stove and chimney flap, and stove use earlier in the trek is documented in the group's diaries [5].\n- The flight pattern — tent slashed from inside, boots left behind, ~1.5 km descent — resembles behavior of disoriented, impaired people [1][5].\n- Forensic notes describing unusual grey-brown/\"sunlamp-tan\" skin on some bodies are claimed to resemble CO's cherry-red lividity [5][7].\n- No carboxyhemoglobin test was performed in the 1959 autopsies, which proponents frame as an evidentiary gap rather than disproof [5].\n\n## Criticisms\n- **Premise contested:** searchers' reports indicate the stove was found disassembled/not set up that night, and diaries record no smoke or fume problems (accounts vary) [5].\n- **Clinical mismatch:** CO intoxication causes gradual incapacitation or collapse, not a coordinated group decision to partially dress, cut the tent, descend together, and later perform purposeful tasks (building a fire under the cedar, climbing it) [1][5].\n- **Explains little:** it does not account for Zolotaryov's crushed ribs or Thibeaux-Brignollel's skull fracture (better explained by avalanche-slab force or falls into the ravine), Dubinina's missing tongue/eyes (scavenging), or the \"tan\" skin (frostbite, sun exposure, and mummification over weeks before recovery are accepted explanations) [1][2][5].\n- The 1959 autopsies found no alcohol; no other toxicological red flags were recorded [5].\n\n## Treatment by investigators and Wikipedia\n- **1959 Soviet inquiry** (Ivanov): closed with \"compelling natural force\"; no criminal cause [1].\n- **2019–2020 Prosecutor General's review** (Andrey Kuryakov, Yekaterinburg): examined dozens of versions (~75 reported), concluded a slab avalanche forced the group out, followed by hypothermia in −25 to −40 °C and poor visibility; explicitly ruled out foul play and dismissed sensational theories (UFOs, yeti, etc.). CO was not among supported causes [3][4].\n- **2021 peer-reviewed modeling** (Gaume & Puzrin, *Communications Earth & Environment*): a delayed slab avalanche on the ~23° slope explains the escape and injuries; the authors note competing hypotheses (katabatic wind, infrasound) lack physical modeling [2].\n- **Wikipedia**: the article foregrounds hypothermia/paradoxical undressing, the avalanche family of theories (including the 2021 study), katabatic wind, infrasound, animals, and military-test speculation, and cites the official 2020 conclusion. The CO/stove hypothesis receives, at most, brief treatment as one of many proposed explanations and is not endorsed or given detailed analysis [1].\n\n**Bottom line:** CO poisoning is a recurring naturalistic hypothesis with superficial appeal for the panic flight, but it lacks a documented trigger (stove use that night is disputed), fits the forensic picture poorly, has no named academic or official champion, and is treated by investigators and Wikipedia as a minor, unsupported hypothesis relative to avalanche + hypothermia [1][2][4].\n\n## Sources\n[1] Wikipedia, \"Dyatlov Pass incident\"\n[2] Gaume & Puzrin (2021), \"Mechanisms of the Dyatlov Pass incident,\" *Communications Earth & Environment* 2:10\n[3] BBC News (Feb 2019), Russia reopens Dyatlov Pass investigation\n[4] Reuters/TASS (July 2020), prosecutors conclude avalanche killed the nine hikers\n[5] Dyatlov-Pass.com / Dyatlov Foundation case-file archive (1959 autopsy and searchers' reports; hypothesis catalogue)\n[6] Donnie Eichar, *Dead Mountain* (2013) — infrasound theory (for contrast)\n[7] Popular press coverage of Dyatlov theories (e.g., The Siberian Times, History.com)\n\n---\n\n## Peer-reviewed follow-ups and critiques of the Gaume-Puzrin Dyatlov Pass slab avalanche model, 2022-2026, including any katabatic wind follow-up paper by Puzrin and Gaume\n# Gaume–Puzrin Dyatlov Pass slab-avalanche model: follow-ups and critiques, 2022–2026\n\n## Baseline: the model in question\nThe only Dyatlov-specific peer-reviewed publication by these authors that I can confirm is the original model paper [1]:\n\n- **Gaume, J. & Puzrin, A.M. (2021).** \"Mechanisms of slab avalanche release and impact in the Dyatlov Pass incident in 1959.\" *Communications Earth & Environment* 2, Article 10. doi:10.1038/s43247-021-00097-5\n- Core mechanism: a **cut/den dug into the slope** (stress concentration) combined with a **wind-deposited snow overload** (attributed to katabatic winds draining off the plateau) on a weak weak layer, producing a **delayed slab release ~7.5–13.5 h later**; the modest slab size was argued to explain the severe blunt-force injuries (rib/skull fractures without external wounds) with little burial and little persistent debris. Finite-element/cohesive-zone modeling was used.\n\n## Direct answer on the katabatic-wind follow-up\n**I cannot confirm any separate peer-reviewed \"katabatic wind\" follow-up paper by Puzrin and Gaume.** The katabatic-wind loading scenario is embedded in the 2021 paper itself; I have no verifiable record of a standalone follow-up through my reliable knowledge horizon (early 2025). Any such paper would have to date from 2025–2026 and should be checked directly (see verification steps below). I deliberately do not cite one, because I cannot verify it exists.\n\n## Peer-reviewed critiques/follow-ups, 2022–2026\n- I am **not aware of a formal comment, Matters Arising, or rebuttal published in *Communications Earth & Environment* or the snow-science journals** (Journal of Glaciology, The Cryosphere, Cold Regions Science and Technology, Annals of Glaciology) responding to the 2021 model, as of my verifiable knowledge.\n- The scientific discussion has taken place largely in **grey literature and media**, not peer-reviewed venues. Recurring technical objections raised there [3][4]:\n  1. Searchers in Feb–Mar 1959 documented **no avalanche debris or fracture line** above the tent.\n  2. The slope at the tent site (measured ~23–25° by investigators; ~28° used in the model) is at or below typical slab-release angles, making results sensitive to assumed parameters.\n  3. The model's inputs (weak-layer depth/thickness, wind-added load, den geometry) were **plausible-range assumptions, not measurements** from the site.\n  4. Debate over whether the injury pattern and the \"orderly\" exit (tent cut from inside, walking footprints) fit an avalanche.\n- Context supporting avalanche plausibility: the Russian Prosecutor General's 2019–2020 re-investigation independently concluded \"avalanche + hypothermia,\" though not via the Gaume–Puzrin mechanism [2].\n\n## How to verify 2025–2026 items (recommended searches)\n- **Google Scholar / Scopus / Web of Science**: `\"Dyatlov\" avalanche` ; `\"Dyatlov Pass\" slab` ; author search **Johan Gaume** (EPFL CRYOS / formerly SLF) and **Alexander M. Puzrin** (ETH Zurich) — check their publication lists and set Scholar alerts for \"Dyatlov.\"\n- **Likely venues** if a follow-up exists: *Communications Earth & Environment*, *Journal of Glaciology*, *The Cryosphere*, *Cold Regions Science and Technology*, and **ISSW proceedings** (International Snow Science Workshop, e.g., ISSW 2024 Tromsø) [5].\n- **Russian-language critique search**: eLibrary.ru / CyberLeninka, terms «перевал Дятлова лавина»; candidate venues: «Лёд и Снег» (Ice and Snow), «Криосфера Земли» (Earth's Cryosphere). I cannot confirm specific Russian journal critiques.\n\n## Caveats\n- I could not run live database queries in this session; the 2021 citation [1] is high-confidence, but **verify the DOI and article number** before citing.\n- Publications from mid-2025 onward are outside my verifiable knowledge; absence of evidence above is not evidence of absence for 2025–2026.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Gaume & Puzrin (2021), *Comm. Earth Environ.* 2:10 — the slab-avalanche model.\n[2] Prosecutor General's Office of the Russian Federation, 2020 re-investigation conclusion (official statement, not peer-reviewed).\n[3] Dyatlovpass.com — detailed technical critique of the slab-avalanche theory (grey literature).\n[4] 2021 media/avalanche-community commentary (e.g., BBC News; practitioner discussions) summarizing the objections above.\n[5] ISSW proceedings archive — most probable venue for any applied follow-up.\n\n---\n\n## Wikipedia Dyatlov Pass incident article: talk page editorial debates, hedging and attribution language in theory sections, and how editors distinguish established conclusions from speculation\n**Scope caveat:** I can't retrieve live pages, so this is based on my knowledge of the article and its talk-page archives as of early 2025. The article is actively edited and may have changed since; verify current wording at the live article and Talk:Dyatlov Pass incident (the talk page has numerous numbered archives — most debate is in Archive 1–5).\n\n## 1. How the article is structured to separate fact from theory\n\nThe article's architecture itself performs the fact/speculation split:\n\n- A chronological narrative section (\"Search\" / \"Investigation\") states the **documented record**: the group's route, the discovery of the abandoned tent (cut open from inside), the trail of footprints, the locations of the bodies, and the post-mortem examination findings (six from hypothermia, three from fatal injuries — Dubinina, Thibault-Brignolles, Zolotaryov, Kolmogorova in various groupings). These statements are written in Wikipedia's own voice, sourced to case-file reporting and reliable secondary sources.\n- A separate **\"Theories and explanations\"** (or \"Explanations\") section contains *only attributed* content. Recurring pattern: \"According to the official investigation…\", \"Investigators concluded…\", \"X proposed…\", \"Y argued…\", \"…has been described as…\", \"…was reported to have…\".\n- The Russian re-investigation (reopened February 2019; conclusions announced July 2020) is presented as an official finding with attribution: the Prosecutor General's office concluded the hikers fled a **slab avalanche** and died of hypothermia; the lead investigator (Andrey Kuryakov) explicitly ruled out criminality and dismissed UFO/paranormal versions. The 2021 *Communications Earth & Environment* paper by **Gaume and Puzrin** (delayed slab-avalanche model) is presented as a peer-reviewed hypothesis — \"demonstrated in a model,\" \"proposed,\" \"consistent with\" — never as the settled answer.\n\n## 2. Hedging and attribution language in the theory sections\n\n- **Attribution verbs** dominate: *concluded, proposed, argued, suggested, hypothesized, attributed, ruled out, concluded that… was the most likely cause*. Theories are named after their proponents (\"A 2021 study by…\", \"…the theory, advanced by…\").\n- **Epistemic hedges** are pervasive: \"most likely,\" \"may have,\" \"could have,\" \"consistent with,\" \"remains disputed,\" \"no consensus.\" The 1959 verdict's own vagueness is preserved via the attributed phrase that the deaths were caused by a \"**compelling natural force**\" (\"стихийная сила, превышающая возможности людей\") — the article notes investigators could not identify a specific cause, rather than filling the gap editorially.\n- **Contested \"facts\" are de-mystified with attribution**: the radiation claim is typically phrased as *reported* elevated activity on some clothing of certain victims, with figures varying between sources — often flagged in text or footnotes as inconsistently reported. The \"cut from inside\" detail is stated as a case-file fact but stripped of the \"as if fleeing in panic\" editorializing.\n- **Due-weight ordering**: official findings first, then peer-reviewed science (Gaume–Puzrin), then scientific minority hypotheses (infrasound/ Kármán vortex street \"Kátabatic wind\" proposals), with the least-supported ideas either absent or compressed to a sentence.\n\n## 3. Recurring talk-page editorial debates\n\nThe archives show a stable set of disputes, all essentially about the fact/speculation boundary:\n\n1. **The lead's framing — \"unexplained\" vs. solved.** Long-running argument over whether the lead may call the deaths \"unexplained\" or \"mysterious,\" or must foreground the official 2020 avalanche conclusion. \"Mystery\" advocates cite overwhelming usage in reliable sources; opponents cite WP:PUZZLE and WP:NOTNEWS, arguing the article shouldn't launder sensationalism into Wikipedia's voice. Post-2020 versions tend to attribute the mystery framing (\"often described as…\") rather than assert it.\n2. **Fringe theories: include, exclude, or quarantine.** UFOs, the Yeti, KGB/cover-up, secret-weapon and \"controlled demolition\"-style theories generate periodic removal requests and WP:FRINGE noticeboard threads. The prevailing resolution: exclude from the theory section entirely, or mention only in \"Legacy\"/\"In popular culture\" as *cultural phenomena* (\"the incident has inspired numerous conspiracy theories…\"), never as competing explanations. Editors cite WP:GEVAL, WP:PARITY, WP:FALSEBALANCE, and WP:CENSOR in both directions.\n3. **Radiation.** A perennial fight: whether to keep it, whether the numbers are exaggerated (reporting ranges from \"above normal\" to wildly divergent figures), and which sources qualify. Neutral phrasing (\"some clothing was reported to show elevated radioactivity\") was the compromise.\n4. **Section naming and ordering.** Debates over \"Theories\" vs. \"Explanations\" vs. \"Proposed explanations,\" whether to split \"Official conclusions\" from \"Scientific hypotheses\" from \"Popular/fringe claims,\" and whether a summary table of theories is appropriate (opponents argue it implies equal validity).\n5. **\"Cut from inside\" and other loaded details.** Disputes over whether the phrase implies mystery; consensus favors plain factual wording with no implication about what the hikers fled.\n6. **The 1959 verdict language.** How to render \"compelling natural force\" — quote vs. paraphrase, and whether to note the vagueness of the original Russian.\n7. **Sources policy.** Heavy reliance on Russian-language materials (case files, autopsy reports = primary sources), popular books with weak reliability (e.g., some English-language trade books), and translation accuracy; editors push for secondary Russian journalism and caution against editors synthesizing from the case files.\n8. **Case status vocabulary.** Whether to call it \"unsolved,\" \"closed,\" or a \"cold case\" — tied to the legal fact that the 1959 case was formally closed, then effectively closed again in 2020.\n\n## 4. The editorial logic, summarized\n\nEditors distinguish **established conclusions** from **speculation** through four consistent mechanisms: (a) *voice* — facts in Wikipedia's voice, all interpretations attributed; (b) *hierarchy of sources* — official findings and peer-reviewed research above journalistic hypotheses above popular speculation; (c) *due weight* — fringe ideas either omitted or framed as cultural legacy; and (d) *hedge density* — the further a claim is from the official record, the more \"proposed/argued/may have\" language surrounds it. The talk page's recurring battles are essentially enforcement actions keeping the article from sliding back into \"unsolved mystery\" genre conventions.\n\n---\n\n## 1959 Soviet Dyatlov Pass investigation: forensic autopsy cause-of-death findings for each hiker, radiation on clothing, and reliability/limitations of the case file as evidence\nI'll research the Dyatlov Pass investigation, starting with the autopsy findings."